<!--
  bb-isolated — the libvirt network a blastbox VM worker attaches to by default.

  THE ABSENCE OF <forward> IS THE WHOLE POINT. That is libvirt's isolated mode: guests on
  this network reach each other and the host, and nothing is forwarded to the physical
  NIC. There is no rule to get wrong and no daemon to fail open — a packet has nowhere to
  go because no path exists, which is the same property the container tier gets from
  docker's `internal` bridge flag.

  DO NOT ADD <forward mode='nat'/>. libvirt's shipped `default` network has exactly that,
  and VmConfig.network used to point at it: a worker with no egress_policy therefore had
  working internet, from the defaults alone, with no host-side rules. VmRuntime refuses
  to boot that pairing now (see _assert_egress_is_governed), and this file is the other
  half of the fix.

  A worker that NEEDS egress gets it the governed way — an egress_policy applied to its
  IP by the rooter (blastbox.host.runtime.libvirt_egress) — not by being placed on a
  network that forwards.

  DHCP is offered because the guest agent is reached over this network and the IP
  reservation scheme (net-update add ip-dhcp-host) writes into it. The range is the
  tier's, not libvirt's default 192.168.122.0/24, so defining this cannot disturb an
  existing `default` network on the same host.

  Install:
      virsh net-define deploy/libvirt/bb-isolated.xml
      virsh net-start bb-isolated
      virsh net-autostart bb-isolated
  Verify it does NOT forward:
      virsh net-dumpxml bb-isolated | grep -c '<forward'     # must print 0
-->
<network>
  <name>bb-isolated</name>
  <bridge name='bb-virbr0' stp='on' delay='0'/>
  <ip address='192.168.221.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
    <dhcp>
      <range start='192.168.221.2' end='192.168.221.254'/>
    </dhcp>
  </ip>
</network>
